---
title: Interactive Charts
description: Choose chart-owned focus or controlled application interactions for pinned detail, scrolling, zooming, and editing.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction should help the reader inspect, navigate, select, or edit semantic\ndata. It should not turn a chart into a second application state system."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts owns nearest-point focus, grouped focus, keyboard point\nnavigation, point selection callbacks, and native structured tooltips. The\napplication owns interactions that change a domain, viewport, persistent\nselection, or product record."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-interaction","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the interaction"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader task"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspect one point or a same-x group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native chart focus and tooltip"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow focus with one rule or crosshair"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data-less "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" mark"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Paint existing geometry for the active datum/group"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" around an ordinary mark"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Synchronize focus or free coordinates between charts"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" controller"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resize, recolor, or fade existing marks during focus"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inline mark "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep rich framework detail open, including another chart"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pinned composed tooltip body"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Navigate a wide schedule without changing its semantic scale"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native horizontal scrolling"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Crop and pan a continuous domain"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled zoom and viewport state"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edit an interval or record"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled direct manipulation plus form input"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":" defines\nthe controlled gesture loop. "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tooltips and Focus"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\ndefines the native inspection path."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"compare-focus-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare focus marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A focused dot can resize and restyle the existing pointer target:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"dot(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y: 'Close',\n  r: 3,\n  fill: '#2563eb',\n  states: [\n    {\n      when: { focus: 'primary' },\n      style: { r: 7, stroke: 'Canvas', strokeWidth: 2 },\n      transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 140, easing: 'ease-out' },\n    },\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same definition can make the built-in indicator smaller while retaining\nits series-colored outline:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"34-pointer-tooltip","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A focused band emphasizes the shared x value for every series. Its position\nbefore the lines places it underneath them:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"marks: [\n  whenFocused(\n    bandX(dates, {\n      x: 'date',\n      fill: '#64748b',\n      fillOpacity: 0.14,\n      inset: 3,\n    }),\n    { match: 'x' },\n  ),\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'unemployed', color: 'industry' }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/35-grouped-tooltip/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the grouped focus example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nto inspect its live chart and complete source."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"follow-focus-with-a-crosshair","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Follow focus with a crosshair"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A crosshair is one dynamic guide driven by the existing focus state. It is not\none hidden rule per datum:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"marks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, { x: 'period', y: 'value', color: 'series', inset: 4 }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It follows pointer and keyboard focus, stays out of hit testing, and renders\nthrough SVG, Canvas, motion, and chart-owned focus presentation. The first guide\nuses categorical bandwidth to paint below the bars; with bar inset 4 and band\ninset 0, it extends 4 pixels past each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused\nperiod. The second guide paints the dotted y rule above the bars and labels the\nfocused stack endpoint. Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maxFocusDistance"},{"type":"text","value":" to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the guides should remain snapped across\nthe complete plot."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/119-stacked-bar-band-cursor/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Open the stacked cursor-band example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nto inspect the live chart and complete source."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For synchronized charts or a free two-dimensional cursor, create one\ncontroller from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/cursor"},{"type":"text","value":" and bind it through definition\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cursor"},{"type":"text","value":". Focus mode shares semantic x/y values and local charts map them to\ntheir own pixels. Free mode shares controlled coordinates without selecting a\ndatum. The complete state and inversion examples are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md#cursors-and-crosshairs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pin-and-expand-rich-detail","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pin and expand rich detail"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This energy tooltip stays compact on hover or keyboard focus. Click, Enter, or\nSpace pins the same surface, adds solar coverage to its native rows, and\nsmoothly reveals the detailed consumption and generation breakdown, including\nan ordinary nested chart. Hover remains chart focus only; framework detail\nmounts after click or keyboard activation."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"84-pinned-nested-chart-tooltip","height":"500"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition owns stable point identity, the pinned mark state,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"visibility: 'pinned'"},{"type":"text","value":", placement, portaling, Escape, and focus return. The\nadapter body receives the pinned point and mounts an ordinary nested chart.\nThe application retains only the same-species cohort policy, close-button\npresentation, and child-chart content."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The definition's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"content"},{"type":"text","value":" callback receives "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pinned"},{"type":"text","value":", so it can keep the\ntransient summary short and add structured rows only after activation. The\nReact "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderTooltipBody"},{"type":"text","value":" callback receives that updated "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defaultBody"},{"type":"text","value":" and the\nsame pinned state:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<TooltipChart\n  definition={definition}\n  renderTooltipBody={({ points, defaultBody, pinned, dismiss }) => (\n    <EnergyTooltip\n      month={points[0].datum}\n      summary={defaultBody}\n      expanded={pinned}\n      onClose={dismiss}\n    />\n  )}\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The detail wrapper stays mounted and transitions from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid-template-rows: 0fr"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1fr"},{"type":"text","value":"; its direct child uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"min-height: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"overflow: hidden"},{"type":"text","value":". This animates intrinsic height without measuring content.\nThe transient body remains inert, controls render only while pinned, and the\nnested consumption chart has its own accessible label and lifecycle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"portal"},{"type":"text","value":" extension to escape clipped ancestors and use viewport\ncollision handling. Wire the close button to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dismiss"},{"type":"text","value":"; the shared host also\nowns Escape, focus return, and non-modal dialog semantics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"scroll-a-wide-schedule","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scroll a wide schedule"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native horizontal scrolling is often better than zoom for resource lanes. It\npreserves a stable time scale and gives the browser proven wheel, touch, and\nkeyboard behavior."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"85-scrollable-resource-lanes","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep lane labels in a fixed rail and place the timeline in the scroll region.\nPreserve lane order, task keys, scroll position, and viewport-relative geometry\nacross data updates. Do not capture vertical page scrolling when the timeline\nonly needs horizontal movement."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Position the external rail from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onRender"},{"type":"text","value":" with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.scales.y.map"},{"type":"text","value":". Do not\nconstruct a second band scale to reproduce chart-space label centers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layout, Axes, and Coordinates"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nto align labels and the plotted region."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"zoom-and-pan-a-time-domain","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zoom and pan a time domain"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" changes a controlled semantic window through the normal definition.\nThe x scale, wheel, drag, touch, keyboard, and reset control all use the same\nstart and end values."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"90-zoomable-time-window","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"zoomX"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom"},{"type":"text","value":", bind its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":" to a\ncontrolled signal, and provide the full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"extent"},{"type":"text","value":" and allowed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scaleExtent"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe behavior owns final-scale inversion, configurable wheel capture,\npointer-anchored zoom, pan, touch and keyboard input, cancellation, clamping,\nand teardown. Wheel capture defaults to focused plots. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"wheelActivation: 'modifier'"},{"type":"text","value":" when Control+wheel or Command+wheel should act\nwithout prior focus while unmodified wheels continue scrolling the page."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the accepted window, visible-row or clipping policy, y-domain policy,\nstatus, reset control, persistence, and follow-latest behavior in application\nstate. Preserve the window when data values update unless product policy\nexplicitly follows the latest point."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"edit-an-interval","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Edit an interval"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An editable timeline combines direct manipulation with native semantic\ncontrols. The chart renders the current record; application validation decides\nwhich edit can commit."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"92-editable-event-range","height":"500"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A complete editor should:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve the event's stable ID, start, and lane while its end changes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clamp the end after the start."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Support pointer cancellation and rollback."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Offer keyboard-adjustable handles with adequate hit targets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide a native date or range input."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Announce the current duration and validation state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep color-independent event labels visible."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not mutate a rectangle and treat that painted geometry as the saved record.\nUpdate application state, validate it, and let the next definition produce the\nscene."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"state-and-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State and lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application-owned interaction state should be semantic:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Date or numeric domain"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selected row ID"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start and end values"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scroll offset"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Playback index"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pinned datum key"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared cursor x/y value"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pixel geometry is derived from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scene.chart"},{"type":"text","value":" and resolved scales on each\nrender. This keeps state valid after responsive layout, font changes, and\nserver hydration."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controllers and overlays may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested hosts, and animation frames. Tear down every resource when\nthe chart unmounts or ownership changes."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent semantic outcomes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus indicators and controls remain visible and contained."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wheel handling does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dragging has commit, cancel, clamp, and out-of-bounds behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selection and viewport survive data and size updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reset behavior is explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native chart focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns\nthe surface."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rich overlays and nested charts have independent accessibility and cleanup."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interaction state is tested as values, not only screenshots or DOM order."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/testing-and-debugging.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Testing and Debugging"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for behavior\nscenarios and "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/accessibility.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accessibility"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for equivalent\ninput paths."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-interaction","text":"Choose the interaction","level":2},{"id":"compare-focus-marks","text":"Compare focus marks","level":2},{"id":"follow-focus-with-a-crosshair","text":"Follow focus with a crosshair","level":2},{"id":"pin-and-expand-rich-detail","text":"Pin and expand rich detail","level":2},{"id":"scroll-a-wide-schedule","text":"Scroll a wide schedule","level":2},{"id":"zoom-and-pan-a-time-domain","text":"Zoom and pan a time domain","level":2},{"id":"edit-an-interval","text":"Edit an interval","level":2},{"id":"state-and-lifecycle","text":"State and lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nInteraction should help the reader inspect, navigate, select, or edit semantic\ndata. It should not turn a chart into a second application state system.\n\nTanStack Charts owns nearest-point focus, grouped focus, keyboard point\nnavigation, point selection callbacks, and native structured tooltips. The\napplication owns interactions that change a domain, viewport, persistent\nselection, or product record.\n\n## Choose the interaction\n\n| Reader task                                                  | Start with                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| Inspect one point or a same-x group                          | Native chart focus and tooltip                 |\n| Follow focus with one rule or crosshair                      | Data-less `crosshair` mark                     |\n| Paint existing geometry for the active datum/group           | `whenFocused` around an ordinary mark          |\n| Synchronize focus or free coordinates between charts         | Shared `createChartCursor` controller          |\n| Resize, recolor, or fade existing marks during focus         | Inline mark `states`                           |\n| Keep rich framework detail open, including another chart     | Pinned composed tooltip body                   |\n| Navigate a wide schedule without changing its semantic scale | Native horizontal scrolling                    |\n| Crop and pan a continuous domain                             | Controlled zoom and viewport state             |\n| Edit an interval or record                                   | Controlled direct manipulation plus form input |\n\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) defines\nthe controlled gesture loop. [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md)\ndefines the native inspection path.\n\n## Compare focus marks\n\nA focused dot can resize and restyle the existing pointer target:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y: 'Close',\n  r: 3,\n  fill: '#2563eb',\n  states: [\n    {\n      when: { focus: 'primary' },\n      style: { r: 7, stroke: 'Canvas', strokeWidth: 2 },\n      transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 140, easing: 'ease-out' },\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe same definition can make the built-in indicator smaller while retaining\nits series-colored outline:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=34-pointer-tooltip height=480 -->\n\nA focused band emphasizes the shared x value for every series. Its position\nbefore the lines places it underneath them:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  whenFocused(\n    bandX(dates, {\n      x: 'date',\n      fill: '#64748b',\n      fillOpacity: 0.14,\n      inset: 3,\n    }),\n    { match: 'x' },\n  ),\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'unemployed', color: 'industry' }),\n]\n```\n\n[Open the grouped focus example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/35-grouped-tooltip/)\nto inspect its live chart and complete source.\n\n## Follow focus with a crosshair\n\nA crosshair is one dynamic guide driven by the existing focus state. It is not\none hidden rule per datum:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, { x: 'period', y: 'value', color: 'series', inset: 4 }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nIt follows pointer and keyboard focus, stays out of hit testing, and renders\nthrough SVG, Canvas, motion, and chart-owned focus presentation. The first guide\nuses categorical bandwidth to paint below the bars; with bar inset 4 and band\ninset 0, it extends 4 pixels past each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused\nperiod. The second guide paints the dotted y rule above the bars and labels the\nfocused stack endpoint. Set `maxFocusDistance` to\n`Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY` only when the guides should remain snapped across\nthe complete plot.\n\n[Open the stacked cursor-band example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/119-stacked-bar-band-cursor/)\nto inspect the live chart and complete source.\n\nFor synchronized charts or a free two-dimensional cursor, create one\ncontroller from `@tanstack/charts/cursor` and bind it through definition\n`cursor`. Focus mode shares semantic x/y values and local charts map them to\ntheir own pixels. Free mode shares controlled coordinates without selecting a\ndatum. The complete state and inversion examples are in\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md#cursors-and-crosshairs).\n\n## Pin and expand rich detail\n\nThis energy tooltip stays compact on hover or keyboard focus. Click, Enter, or\nSpace pins the same surface, adds solar coverage to its native rows, and\nsmoothly reveals the detailed consumption and generation breakdown, including\nan ordinary nested chart. Hover remains chart focus only; framework detail\nmounts after click or keyboard activation.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=84-pinned-nested-chart-tooltip height=500 -->\n\nThe definition owns stable point identity, the pinned mark state,\n`visibility: 'pinned'`, placement, portaling, Escape, and focus return. The\nadapter body receives the pinned point and mounts an ordinary nested chart.\nThe application retains only the same-species cohort policy, close-button\npresentation, and child-chart content.\n\nThe definition's `content` callback receives `pinned`, so it can keep the\ntransient summary short and add structured rows only after activation. The\nReact `renderTooltipBody` callback receives that updated `defaultBody` and the\nsame pinned state:\n\n```tsx\n<TooltipChart\n  definition={definition}\n  renderTooltipBody={({ points, defaultBody, pinned, dismiss }) => (\n    <EnergyTooltip\n      month={points[0].datum}\n      summary={defaultBody}\n      expanded={pinned}\n      onClose={dismiss}\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\nThe detail wrapper stays mounted and transitions from\n`grid-template-rows: 0fr` to `1fr`; its direct child uses `min-height: 0` and\n`overflow: hidden`. This animates intrinsic height without measuring content.\nThe transient body remains inert, controls render only while pinned, and the\nnested consumption chart has its own accessible label and lifecycle.\n\nAdd the `portal` extension to escape clipped ancestors and use viewport\ncollision handling. Wire the close button to `dismiss`; the shared host also\nowns Escape, focus return, and non-modal dialog semantics.\n\n## Scroll a wide schedule\n\nNative horizontal scrolling is often better than zoom for resource lanes. It\npreserves a stable time scale and gives the browser proven wheel, touch, and\nkeyboard behavior.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=85-scrollable-resource-lanes height=480 -->\n\nKeep lane labels in a fixed rail and place the timeline in the scroll region.\nPreserve lane order, task keys, scroll position, and viewport-relative geometry\nacross data updates. Do not capture vertical page scrolling when the timeline\nonly needs horizontal movement.\n\nPosition the external rail from `onRender` with `scene.scales.y.map`. Do not\nconstruct a second band scale to reproduce chart-space label centers.\n\nUse [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md)\nto align labels and the plotted region.\n\n## Zoom and pan a time domain\n\n`zoomX` changes a controlled semantic window through the normal definition.\nThe x scale, wheel, drag, touch, keyboard, and reset control all use the same\nstart and end values.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=90-zoomable-time-window height=480 -->\n\nImport `zoomX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom`, bind its `window` to a\ncontrolled signal, and provide the full `extent` and allowed `scaleExtent`.\nThe behavior owns final-scale inversion, configurable wheel capture,\npointer-anchored zoom, pan, touch and keyboard input, cancellation, clamping,\nand teardown. Wheel capture defaults to focused plots. Use\n`wheelActivation: 'modifier'` when Control+wheel or Command+wheel should act\nwithout prior focus while unmodified wheels continue scrolling the page.\n\nKeep the accepted window, visible-row or clipping policy, y-domain policy,\nstatus, reset control, persistence, and follow-latest behavior in application\nstate. Preserve the window when data values update unless product policy\nexplicitly follows the latest point.\n\n## Edit an interval\n\nAn editable timeline combines direct manipulation with native semantic\ncontrols. The chart renders the current record; application validation decides\nwhich edit can commit.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=92-editable-event-range height=500 -->\n\nA complete editor should:\n\n- Preserve the event's stable ID, start, and lane while its end changes.\n- Clamp the end after the start.\n- Support pointer cancellation and rollback.\n- Offer keyboard-adjustable handles with adequate hit targets.\n- Provide a native date or range input.\n- Announce the current duration and validation state.\n- Keep color-independent event labels visible.\n\nDo not mutate a rectangle and treat that painted geometry as the saved record.\nUpdate application state, validate it, and let the next definition produce the\nscene.\n\n## State and lifecycle\n\nApplication-owned interaction state should be semantic:\n\n- Date or numeric domain\n- Selected row ID\n- Start and end values\n- Scroll offset\n- Playback index\n- Pinned datum key\n- Shared cursor x/y value\n\nPixel geometry is derived from `scene.chart` and resolved scales on each\nrender. This keeps state valid after responsive layout, font changes, and\nserver hydration.\n\nControllers and overlays may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested hosts, and animation frames. Tear down every resource when\nthe chart unmounts or ownership changes.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent semantic outcomes.\n- Focus indicators and controls remain visible and contained.\n- Wheel handling does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling.\n- Dragging has commit, cancel, clamp, and out-of-bounds behavior.\n- Selection and viewport survive data and size updates.\n- Reset behavior is explicit.\n- Native chart focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns\n  the surface.\n- Rich overlays and nested charts have independent accessibility and cleanup.\n- Interaction state is tested as values, not only screenshots or DOM order.\n\nUse [Testing and Debugging](../guides/testing-and-debugging.md) for behavior\nscenarios and [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md) for equivalent\ninput paths.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Interactive Charts\ndescription: Choose chart-owned focus or controlled application interactions for pinned detail, scrolling, zooming, and editing.\n---\n\nInteraction should help the reader inspect, navigate, select, or edit semantic\ndata. It should not turn a chart into a second application state system.\n\nTanStack Charts owns nearest-point focus, grouped focus, keyboard point\nnavigation, point selection callbacks, and native structured tooltips. The\napplication owns interactions that change a domain, viewport, persistent\nselection, or product record.\n\n## Choose the interaction\n\n| Reader task                                                  | Start with                                     |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| Inspect one point or a same-x group                          | Native chart focus and tooltip                 |\n| Follow focus with one rule or crosshair                      | Data-less `crosshair` mark                     |\n| Paint existing geometry for the active datum/group           | `whenFocused` around an ordinary mark          |\n| Synchronize focus or free coordinates between charts         | Shared `createChartCursor` controller          |\n| Resize, recolor, or fade existing marks during focus         | Inline mark `states`                           |\n| Keep rich framework detail open, including another chart     | Pinned composed tooltip body                   |\n| Navigate a wide schedule without changing its semantic scale | Native horizontal scrolling                    |\n| Crop and pan a continuous domain                             | Controlled zoom and viewport state             |\n| Edit an interval or record                                   | Controlled direct manipulation plus form input |\n\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) defines\nthe controlled gesture loop. [Tooltips and Focus](../guides/tooltips-and-focus.md)\ndefines the native inspection path.\n\n## Compare focus marks\n\nA focused dot can resize and restyle the existing pointer target:\n\n```ts\ndot(rows, {\n  x: 'Date',\n  y: 'Close',\n  r: 3,\n  fill: '#2563eb',\n  states: [\n    {\n      when: { focus: 'primary' },\n      style: { r: 7, stroke: 'Canvas', strokeWidth: 2 },\n      transition: { type: 'tween', duration: 140, easing: 'ease-out' },\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe same definition can make the built-in indicator smaller while retaining\nits series-colored outline:\n\n```ts\nconst definition = defineChart({\n  marks,\n  scales,\n  focusRing: {\n    radius: 4,\n    strokeWidth: 2,\n    fill: '#ffffff',\n  },\n})\n```\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=34-pointer-tooltip height=480 -->\n\nA focused band emphasizes the shared x value for every series. Its position\nbefore the lines places it underneath them:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  whenFocused(\n    bandX(dates, {\n      x: 'date',\n      fill: '#64748b',\n      fillOpacity: 0.14,\n      inset: 3,\n    }),\n    { match: 'x' },\n  ),\n  lineY(rows, { x: 'date', y: 'unemployed', color: 'industry' }),\n]\n```\n\n[Open the grouped focus example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/35-grouped-tooltip/)\nto inspect its live chart and complete source.\n\n## Follow focus with a crosshair\n\nA crosshair is one dynamic guide driven by the existing focus state. It is not\none hidden rule per datum:\n\n```ts\nmarks: [\n  crosshair({\n    x: {\n      band: {\n        inset: 0,\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: '#64748b',\n        fillOpacity: 0.16,\n      },\n      label: true,\n    },\n    y: false,\n  }),\n  barY(rows, { x: 'period', y: 'value', color: 'series', inset: 4 }),\n  crosshair({\n    x: false,\n    y: { strokeDasharray: '4 4', label: true },\n  }),\n]\n```\n\nIt follows pointer and keyboard focus, stays out of hit testing, and renders\nthrough SVG, Canvas, motion, and chart-owned focus presentation. The first guide\nuses categorical bandwidth to paint below the bars; with bar inset 4 and band\ninset 0, it extends 4 pixels past each bar edge. Its x label shows the focused\nperiod. The second guide paints the dotted y rule above the bars and labels the\nfocused stack endpoint. Set `maxFocusDistance` to\n`Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY` only when the guides should remain snapped across\nthe complete plot.\n\n[Open the stacked cursor-band example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/119-stacked-bar-band-cursor/)\nto inspect the live chart and complete source.\n\nFor synchronized charts or a free two-dimensional cursor, create one\ncontroller from `@tanstack/charts/cursor` and bind it through definition\n`cursor`. Focus mode shares semantic x/y values and local charts map them to\ntheir own pixels. Free mode shares controlled coordinates without selecting a\ndatum. The complete state and inversion examples are in\n[Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md#cursors-and-crosshairs).\n\n## Pin and expand rich detail\n\nThis energy tooltip stays compact on hover or keyboard focus. Click, Enter, or\nSpace pins the same surface, adds solar coverage to its native rows, and\nsmoothly reveals the detailed consumption and generation breakdown, including\nan ordinary nested chart. Hover remains chart focus only; framework detail\nmounts after click or keyboard activation.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=84-pinned-nested-chart-tooltip height=500 -->\n\nThe definition owns stable point identity, the pinned mark state,\n`visibility: 'pinned'`, placement, portaling, Escape, and focus return. The\nadapter body receives the pinned point and mounts an ordinary nested chart.\nThe application retains only the same-species cohort policy, close-button\npresentation, and child-chart content.\n\nThe definition's `content` callback receives `pinned`, so it can keep the\ntransient summary short and add structured rows only after activation. The\nReact `renderTooltipBody` callback receives that updated `defaultBody` and the\nsame pinned state:\n\n```tsx\n<TooltipChart\n  definition={definition}\n  renderTooltipBody={({ points, defaultBody, pinned, dismiss }) => (\n    <EnergyTooltip\n      month={points[0].datum}\n      summary={defaultBody}\n      expanded={pinned}\n      onClose={dismiss}\n    />\n  )}\n/>\n```\n\nThe detail wrapper stays mounted and transitions from\n`grid-template-rows: 0fr` to `1fr`; its direct child uses `min-height: 0` and\n`overflow: hidden`. This animates intrinsic height without measuring content.\nThe transient body remains inert, controls render only while pinned, and the\nnested consumption chart has its own accessible label and lifecycle.\n\nAdd the `portal` extension to escape clipped ancestors and use viewport\ncollision handling. Wire the close button to `dismiss`; the shared host also\nowns Escape, focus return, and non-modal dialog semantics.\n\n## Scroll a wide schedule\n\nNative horizontal scrolling is often better than zoom for resource lanes. It\npreserves a stable time scale and gives the browser proven wheel, touch, and\nkeyboard behavior.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=85-scrollable-resource-lanes height=480 -->\n\nKeep lane labels in a fixed rail and place the timeline in the scroll region.\nPreserve lane order, task keys, scroll position, and viewport-relative geometry\nacross data updates. Do not capture vertical page scrolling when the timeline\nonly needs horizontal movement.\n\nPosition the external rail from `onRender` with `scene.scales.y.map`. Do not\nconstruct a second band scale to reproduce chart-space label centers.\n\nUse [Layout, Axes, and Coordinates](../concepts/layout-axes-and-coordinates.md)\nto align labels and the plotted region.\n\n## Zoom and pan a time domain\n\n`zoomX` changes a controlled semantic window through the normal definition.\nThe x scale, wheel, drag, touch, keyboard, and reset control all use the same\nstart and end values.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=90-zoomable-time-window height=480 -->\n\nImport `zoomX` from `@tanstack/charts/interaction/zoom`, bind its `window` to a\ncontrolled signal, and provide the full `extent` and allowed `scaleExtent`.\nThe behavior owns final-scale inversion, configurable wheel capture,\npointer-anchored zoom, pan, touch and keyboard input, cancellation, clamping,\nand teardown. Wheel capture defaults to focused plots. Use\n`wheelActivation: 'modifier'` when Control+wheel or Command+wheel should act\nwithout prior focus while unmodified wheels continue scrolling the page.\n\nKeep the accepted window, visible-row or clipping policy, y-domain policy,\nstatus, reset control, persistence, and follow-latest behavior in application\nstate. Preserve the window when data values update unless product policy\nexplicitly follows the latest point.\n\n## Edit an interval\n\nAn editable timeline combines direct manipulation with native semantic\ncontrols. The chart renders the current record; application validation decides\nwhich edit can commit.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=92-editable-event-range height=500 -->\n\nA complete editor should:\n\n- Preserve the event's stable ID, start, and lane while its end changes.\n- Clamp the end after the start.\n- Support pointer cancellation and rollback.\n- Offer keyboard-adjustable handles with adequate hit targets.\n- Provide a native date or range input.\n- Announce the current duration and validation state.\n- Keep color-independent event labels visible.\n\nDo not mutate a rectangle and treat that painted geometry as the saved record.\nUpdate application state, validate it, and let the next definition produce the\nscene.\n\n## State and lifecycle\n\nApplication-owned interaction state should be semantic:\n\n- Date or numeric domain\n- Selected row ID\n- Start and end values\n- Scroll offset\n- Playback index\n- Pinned datum key\n- Shared cursor x/y value\n\nPixel geometry is derived from `scene.chart` and resolved scales on each\nrender. This keeps state valid after responsive layout, font changes, and\nserver hydration.\n\nControllers and overlays may install pointer capture, event listeners,\nobservers, nested hosts, and animation frames. Tear down every resource when\nthe chart unmounts or ownership changes.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Pointer, keyboard, and touch reach equivalent semantic outcomes.\n- Focus indicators and controls remain visible and contained.\n- Wheel handling does not unexpectedly trap page scrolling.\n- Dragging has commit, cancel, clamp, and out-of-bounds behavior.\n- Selection and viewport survive data and size updates.\n- Reset behavior is explicit.\n- Native chart focus is disabled only when another complete interaction owns\n  the surface.\n- Rich overlays and nested charts have independent accessibility and cleanup.\n- Interaction state is tested as values, not only screenshots or DOM order.\n\nUse [Testing and Debugging](../guides/testing-and-debugging.md) for behavior\nscenarios and [Accessibility](../guides/accessibility.md) for equivalent\ninput paths.\n"}
